The scatter plot below shows 6 students plotted by (Study Hours, Sleep Hours). By visual inspection, how many natural groups do you see?
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\draw[->,thick] (-0.5,0) -- (6,0) node[right]{\small Study Hours};
\draw[->,thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,5.5) node[above]{\small Sleep Hours};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (1,4.5) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[primaryblue] (1.4,4.8) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[primaryblue] (0.8,4.2) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[accentorange] (4.5,1.2) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[accentorange] (5,1.5) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[accentorange] (4.8,0.9) circle (3pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Solution:}
The three blue points near the top-left are all close to one another, and the three orange points near the bottom-right are all close to one another, but the two groups are far apart from each other.
\boxed{\text{2 clusters: \{top-left group\}, \{bottom-right group\}}}

The scatter plot below shows 6 shops plotted by (Location X, Location Y). How many natural groups do you see?
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\draw[->,thick] (-0.5,0) -- (6,0) node[right]{\small X};
\draw[->,thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,5.5) node[above]{\small Y};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (0.8,0.8) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[primaryblue] (1.2,0.5) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[accentorange] (5,0.8) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[accentorange] (5.3,1.1) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[darkgreen] (2.8,4.8) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[darkgreen] (3.2,4.5) circle (3pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Solution:}
Three separate pairs of points are visible, each pair close together but far from the other pairs: one pair near the bottom-left, one pair near the bottom-right, and one pair near the top-middle.
\boxed{\text{3 clusters, 2 points each}}

The scatter plot below shows 5 points spaced almost evenly along a line. How many natural clusters do you see?
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\draw[->,thick] (-0.5,0) -- (6,0) node[right]{\small X};
\draw[->,thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,2) node[above]{\small Y};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (1,1) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[primaryblue] (2,1) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[primaryblue] (3,1) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[primaryblue] (4,1) circle (3pt);
\filldraw[primaryblue] (5,1) circle (3pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Solution:}
Every point is roughly equally spaced from its neighbours, so there is no clear gap separating one group from another.
\boxed{\text{No clear natural clusters --- the points form one continuous chain}}

\textbf{Supervised vs Unsupervised Learning:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item \textbf{Supervised} (Decision Trees, Naive Bayes, SVM, Regression): training data has known class labels
\item \textbf{Unsupervised} (Clustering): training data has \textbf{no} labels; the algorithm groups examples by similarity alone
\item A good clustering keeps points within the same cluster close together while keeping different clusters far apart, as seen in Examples 3.1 and 3.2
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Distance Measures}

The Euclidean distance between two points $x=(x_1,x_2)$ and $y=(y_1,y_2)$ measures straight-line closeness:
\[
d(x,y) = \sqrt{(x_1-y_1)^2 + (x_2-y_2)^2}
\]

This is exactly the Pythagorean theorem: the distance is the hypotenuse of a right triangle formed by the horizontal gap and the vertical gap between the two points.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\draw[->,thick] (-0.5,0) -- (5,0) node[right]{\small $x_1$};
\draw[->,thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,4) node[above]{\small $x_2$};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (0.5,0.5) circle (2.5pt) node[below left,font=\scriptsize]{$x$};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (3.5,3.2) circle (2.5pt) node[above right,font=\scriptsize]{$y$};
\draw[accentorange,thick] (0.5,0.5) -- (3.5,3.2);
\draw[darkgray,dashed] (0.5,0.5) -- (3.5,0.5) node[midway,below,font=\scriptsize]{$|x_1-y_1|$};
\draw[darkgray,dashed] (3.5,0.5) -- (3.5,3.2) node[midway,right,font=\scriptsize]{$|x_2-y_2|$};
\node[accentorange,font=\scriptsize] at (1.6,2.3) {$d(x,y)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

Compute the Euclidean distance between $(1,2)$ and $(4,6)$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
d &= \sqrt{(1-4)^2+(2-6)^2} = \sqrt{(-3)^2+(-4)^2} = \sqrt{9+16} = \sqrt{25}
\end{align*}
\boxed{d = 5}

Compute the Euclidean distance between $(0,0)$ and $(6,8)$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
d &= \sqrt{(0-6)^2+(0-8)^2} = \sqrt{36+64} = \sqrt{100}
\end{align*}
\boxed{d = 10}

Compute the Euclidean distance between $(2,3)$ and $(2,7)$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
d &= \sqrt{(2-2)^2+(3-7)^2} = \sqrt{0+16} = \sqrt{16}
\end{align*}
\boxed{d = 4}

\textbf{Distance Facts:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item $d(x,y) = 0$ only when $x$ and $y$ are the same point
\item $d(x,y) = d(y,x)$ always \hfill\textit{(symmetry)}
\item Smaller distance means the two points are more similar; clustering always groups the smallest-distance points first
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Linkage Methods Between Clusters}

Given two clusters $A$ and $B$, the distance between them can be defined in three ways:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item \textbf{Single Linkage:} $d(A,B) = \min\limits_{a\in A,\,b\in B} d(a,b)$ \hfill\textit{(closest pair)}
\item \textbf{Complete Linkage:} $d(A,B) = \max\limits_{a\in A,\,b\in B} d(a,b)$ \hfill\textit{(farthest pair)}
\item \textbf{Average Linkage:} $d(A,B) = \dfrac{1}{|A||B|}\sum\limits_{a\in A,\,b\in B} d(a,b)$ \hfill\textit{(mean of all pairs)}
\end{enumerate}

Cluster $A=\{(1,1),(3,2)\}$ and Cluster $B=\{(6,1),(7,3)\}$. Compute the single linkage distance $d(A,B)$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
d((1,1),(6,1)) &= \sqrt{25+0} = 5 \\
d((1,1),(7,3)) &= \sqrt{36+4} = \sqrt{40} \approx 6.325 \\
d((3,2),(6,1)) &= \sqrt{9+1} = \sqrt{10} \approx 3.162 \\
d((3,2),(7,3)) &= \sqrt{16+1} = \sqrt{17} \approx 4.123
\end{align*}
Single linkage takes the \textbf{minimum} of these four values:
\boxed{d(A,B) \approx 3.162}

Cluster $A=\{(0,0),(1,2)\}$ and Cluster $B=\{(4,0),(5,3)\}$. Compute the complete linkage distance $d(A,B)$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
d((0,0),(4,0)) &= 4 \\
d((0,0),(5,3)) &= \sqrt{25+9} = \sqrt{34} \approx 5.831 \\
d((1,2),(4,0)) &= \sqrt{9+4} = \sqrt{13} \approx 3.606 \\
d((1,2),(5,3)) &= \sqrt{16+1} = \sqrt{17} \approx 4.123
\end{align*}
Complete linkage takes the \textbf{maximum} of these four values:
\boxed{d(A,B) \approx 5.831}

Cluster $A=\{(2,2),(3,4)\}$ and Cluster $B=\{(6,2),(7,5)\}$. Compute the average linkage distance $d(A,B)$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
d((2,2),(6,2)) &= 4 \\
d((2,2),(7,5)) &= \sqrt{25+9} = \sqrt{34} \approx 5.831 \\
d((3,4),(6,2)) &= \sqrt{9+4} = \sqrt{13} \approx 3.606 \\
d((3,4),(7,5)) &= \sqrt{16+1} = \sqrt{17} \approx 4.123 \\[4pt]
d(A,B) &= \frac{4+5.831+3.606+4.123}{4} = \frac{17.560}{4}
\end{align*}
\boxed{d(A,B) \approx 4.390}

\subsection{The Agglomerative Clustering Algorithm}

Cluster the points $P_1=(1,1)$, $P_2=(2,1)$, $P_3=(5,1)$, $P_4=(6.5,1)$ using single linkage. Show every merge and draw the dendrogram.

\textbf{Solution:}
Since all points lie on the line $y=1$, distance is just the difference in $x$-coordinates.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
\toprule
 & $P_1$ & $P_2$ & $P_3$ & $P_4$ \\
\midrule
$P_1$ & -- & 1 & 4 & 5.5 \\
$P_2$ & 1 & -- & 3 & 4.5 \\
$P_3$ & 4 & 3 & -- & 1.5 \\
$P_4$ & 5.5 & 4.5 & 1.5 & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\textbf{Step 1:} Smallest distance is $d(P_1,P_2)=1$. Merge into $\{P_1,P_2\}$.
\begin{align*}
d(\{P_1,P_2\},P_3) &= \min(4,3) = 3 \\
d(\{P_1,P_2\},P_4) &= \min(5.5,4.5) = 4.5 \\
d(P_3,P_4) &= 1.5
\end{align*}
\textbf{Step 2:} Smallest distance is now $d(P_3,P_4)=1.5$. Merge into $\{P_3,P_4\}$.
\[
d(\{P_1,P_2\},\{P_3,P_4\}) = \min(4,5.5,3,4.5) = 3
\]
\textbf{Step 3:} Only two clusters remain; merge them at height $3$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=1]
\foreach \x/\lab in {1/$P_1$,2/$P_2$,3/$P_3$,4/$P_4$}
  \node[below,font=\small] at (\x,0) {\lab};
\draw[thick] (1,0) -- (1,1) -- (2,1) -- (2,0);
\node[above,font=\scriptsize] at (1.5,1) {1};
\draw[thick] (3,0) -- (3,1.5) -- (4,1.5) -- (4,0);
\node[above,font=\scriptsize] at (3.5,1.5) {1.5};
\draw[thick] (1.5,1) -- (1.5,3) -- (3.5,3) -- (3.5,1.5);
\node[above,font=\scriptsize] at (2.5,3) {3};
\draw[->,thick] (0,0) -- (0,3.5) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{Distance};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
\boxed{\text{Merge order: } (P_1,P_2)\text{ at }1,\ (P_3,P_4)\text{ at }1.5,\ \text{root at }3}

Cluster the points $Q_1=(1,1)$, $Q_2=(2,3)$, $Q_3=(6,2)$, $Q_4=(7,5)$ using complete linkage. Show every merge and draw the dendrogram.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
d(Q_1,Q_2) &= \sqrt{1+4}\approx 2.236, & d(Q_1,Q_3) &= \sqrt{25+1}\approx 5.099 \\
d(Q_1,Q_4) &= \sqrt{36+16}\approx 7.211, & d(Q_2,Q_3) &= \sqrt{16+1}\approx 4.123 \\
d(Q_2,Q_4) &= \sqrt{25+4}\approx 5.385, & d(Q_3,Q_4) &= \sqrt{1+9}\approx 3.162
\end{align*}
\textbf{Step 1:} Smallest distance is $d(Q_1,Q_2)\approx 2.236$. Merge into $\{Q_1,Q_2\}$.
\begin{align*}
d(\{Q_1,Q_2\},Q_3) &= \max(5.099,4.123) = 5.099 \\
d(\{Q_1,Q_2\},Q_4) &= \max(7.211,5.385) = 7.211 \\
d(Q_3,Q_4) &= 3.162
\end{align*}
\textbf{Step 2:} Smallest distance is now $d(Q_3,Q_4)\approx 3.162$. Merge into $\{Q_3,Q_4\}$.
\[
d(\{Q_1,Q_2\},\{Q_3,Q_4\}) = \max(5.099,7.211,4.123,5.385) = 7.211
\]
\textbf{Step 3:} Only two clusters remain; merge them at height $\approx 7.211$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=0.45]
\foreach \x/\lab in {1/$Q_1$,2/$Q_2$,3/$Q_3$,4/$Q_4$}
  \node[below,font=\small] at (\x,0) {\lab};
\draw[thick] (1,0) -- (1,2.236) -- (2,2.236) -- (2,0);
\node[above,font=\scriptsize] at (1.5,2.236) {2.236};
\draw[thick] (3,0) -- (3,3.162) -- (4,3.162) -- (4,0);
\node[above,font=\scriptsize] at (3.5,3.162) {3.162};
\draw[thick] (1.5,2.236) -- (1.5,7.211) -- (3.5,7.211) -- (3.5,3.162);
\node[above,font=\scriptsize] at (2.5,7.211) {7.211};
\draw[->,thick] (0,0) -- (0,8) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{Distance};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
\boxed{\text{Merge order: } (Q_1,Q_2)\text{ at }2.236,\ (Q_3,Q_4)\text{ at }3.162,\ \text{root at }7.211}

Cluster the points $R_1=(0,0)$, $R_2=(1,1)$, $R_3=(4,0)$, $R_4=(5,2)$ using average linkage. Show every merge and draw the dendrogram.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
d(R_1,R_2) &= \sqrt{1+1}\approx 1.414, & d(R_1,R_3) &= 4 \\
d(R_1,R_4) &= \sqrt{25+4}\approx 5.385, & d(R_2,R_3) &= \sqrt{9+1}\approx 3.162 \\
d(R_2,R_4) &= \sqrt{16+1}\approx 4.123, & d(R_3,R_4) &= \sqrt{1+4}\approx 2.236
\end{align*}
\textbf{Step 1:} Smallest distance is $d(R_1,R_2)\approx 1.414$. Merge into $\{R_1,R_2\}$.
\begin{align*}
d(\{R_1,R_2\},R_3) &= \frac{4+3.162}{2} = 3.581 \\
d(\{R_1,R_2\},R_4) &= \frac{5.385+4.123}{2} = 4.754 \\
d(R_3,R_4) &= 2.236
\end{align*}
\textbf{Step 2:} Smallest distance is now $d(R_3,R_4)\approx 2.236$. Merge into $\{R_3,R_4\}$.
\[
d(\{R_1,R_2\},\{R_3,R_4\}) = \frac{4+5.385+3.162+4.123}{4} = \frac{16.670}{4} \approx 4.168
\]
\textbf{Step 3:} Only two clusters remain; merge them at height $\approx 4.168$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[yscale=0.75]
\foreach \x/\lab in {1/$R_1$,2/$R_2$,3/$R_3$,4/$R_4$}
  \node[below,font=\small] at (\x,0) {\lab};
\draw[thick] (1,0) -- (1,1.414) -- (2,1.414) -- (2,0);
\node[above,font=\scriptsize] at (1.5,1.414) {1.414};
\draw[thick] (3,0) -- (3,2.236) -- (4,2.236) -- (4,0);
\node[above,font=\scriptsize] at (3.5,2.236) {2.236};
\draw[thick] (1.5,1.414) -- (1.5,4.168) -- (3.5,4.168) -- (3.5,2.236);
\node[above,font=\scriptsize] at (2.5,4.168) {4.168};
\draw[->,thick] (0,0) -- (0,5) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{Distance};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
\boxed{\text{Merge order: } (R_1,R_2)\text{ at }1.414,\ (R_3,R_4)\text{ at }2.236,\ \text{root at }4.168}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item k-Means minimizes the \textbf{within-cluster sum of squared distances} between points and their cluster centroid \hfill\textit{(objective)}
\item The number of clusters $k$ must be chosen before running the algorithm \hfill\textit{(fixed $k$)}
\item k-Means always converges (the assignments eventually stop changing), but only to a \textbf{local} optimum, not necessarily the best possible clustering \hfill\textit{(local convergence)}
\item The final clustering depends on the \textbf{initial choice of centroids}; different starting points can give different results \hfill\textit{(initialization sensitivity)}
\end{enumerate}

Cluster the points $A(1,1)$, $B(2,1)$, $C(4,3)$, $D(5,4)$ using $k=2$ with initial centroids $c_1=(1,1)$ and $c_2=(5,4)$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\textbf{Iteration 1 — Assign:}
\begin{align*}
d(A,c_1)=0,\ d(A,c_2)=5 &\ \Rightarrow\ A\to c_1 \\
d(B,c_1)=1,\ d(B,c_2)\approx4.243 &\ \Rightarrow\ B\to c_1 \\
d(C,c_1)\approx3.606,\ d(C,c_2)\approx1.414 &\ \Rightarrow\ C\to c_2 \\
d(D,c_1)=5,\ d(D,c_2)=0 &\ \Rightarrow\ D\to c_2
\end{align*}
\textbf{Update:}
\[
c_1=\left(\frac{1+2}{2},\frac{1+1}{2}\right)=(1.5,1), \qquad c_2=\left(\frac{4+5}{2},\frac{3+4}{2}\right)=(4.5,3.5)
\]
\textbf{Iteration 2 — Assign:} Checking distances to the new centroids, $A$ and $B$ remain closest to $c_1$, and $C$ and $D$ remain closest to $c_2$. No point changes cluster, so the centroids do not move again.
\boxed{\text{Converged: }\{A,B\}\to(1.5,1),\ \{C,D\}\to(4.5,3.5)}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->,thick] (-0.5,0) -- (6.5,0) node[right]{\small $x_1$};
\draw[->,thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,5) node[above]{\small $x_2$};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (1,1) circle (3pt) node[below,font=\scriptsize]{$A$};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (2,1) circle (3pt) node[below,font=\scriptsize]{$B$};
\filldraw[accentorange] (4,3) circle (3pt) node[below,font=\scriptsize]{$C$};
\filldraw[accentorange] (5,4) circle (3pt) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{$D$};
\draw[primaryblue,thick] (1.5,1) circle (5pt);
\node[primaryblue,font=\scriptsize] at (1.5,1.6) {$c_1$};
\draw[accentorange,thick] (4.5,3.5) circle (5pt);
\node[accentorange,font=\scriptsize] at (4.5,4.1) {$c_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

Cluster the points $A(1,1)$, $B(2,2)$, $C(3,3)$, $D(8,8)$, $E(9,9)$ using $k=2$ with initial centroids $c_1=(1,1)$ and $c_2=(2,2)$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\textbf{Iteration 1 — Assign:}
\begin{align*}
A\to c_1\ (d=0<1.414),\quad B\to c_2\ (d=0<1.414),\quad C\to c_2\ (1.414<2.828) \\
D\to c_2\ (8.485<9.899),\quad E\to c_2\ (9.899<11.314)
\end{align*}
\textbf{Update:} $c_1=(1,1)$ (only $A$); $c_2=\left(\frac{2+3+8+9}{4},\frac{2+3+8+9}{4}\right)=(5.5,5.5)$

\textbf{Iteration 2 — Assign (using new centroids):}
\begin{align*}
A\to c_1\ (0<6.364),\quad B\to c_1\ (1.414<4.950)\ \textbf{[flipped]},\quad C\to c_1\ (2.828<3.536)\ \textbf{[flipped]} \\
D\to c_2\ (9.899>3.536),\quad E\to c_2\ (11.314>4.950)
\end{align*}
\textbf{Update:} $c_1=\left(\frac{1+2+3}{3},\frac{1+2+3}{3}\right)=(2,2)$; $c_2=(8.5,8.5)$

\textbf{Iteration 3 — Assign (using new centroids):} $A,B,C$ all remain closest to $c_1$; $D,E$ remain closest to $c_2$. No further changes.
\boxed{\text{Converged: }\{A,B,C\}\to(2,2),\ \{D,E\}\to(8.5,8.5)}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw[->,thick] (-0.5,0) -- (10.5,0) node[right]{\small $x_1$};
\draw[->,thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,10) node[above]{\small $x_2$};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (1,1) circle (3pt) node[below,font=\scriptsize]{$A$};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (2,2) circle (3pt) node[below,font=\scriptsize]{$B$};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (3,3) circle (3pt) node[below,font=\scriptsize]{$C$};
\filldraw[accentorange] (8,8) circle (3pt) node[below,font=\scriptsize]{$D$};
\filldraw[accentorange] (9,9) circle (3pt) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{$E$};
\draw[primaryblue,thick] (2,2) circle (6pt);
\node[primaryblue,font=\scriptsize] at (2,1.2) {final $c_1$};
\draw[accentorange,thick] (8.5,8.5) circle (6pt);
\node[accentorange,font=\scriptsize] at (8.5,9.3) {final $c_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
Notice how the poor initial choice ($c_1,c_2$ starting right next to each other) caused $B$ and $C$ to be misassigned in Iteration 1 and only get corrected in later iterations — this is exactly the initialization sensitivity described in the theorembox above. \checkmark

Cluster the points $A(1,1)$, $B(1,2)$, $C(8,1)$, $D(8,2)$, $E(4,8)$, $F(5,9)$ using $k=3$ with initial centroids $c_1=(1,1)$, $c_2=(8,1)$, $c_3=(4,8)$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\textbf{Iteration 1 — Assign:}
\begin{align*}
A\to c_1\ (0),\quad B\to c_1\ (1<7.071,6.708),\quad C\to c_2\ (0),\quad D\to c_2\ (1<7.211) \\
E\to c_3\ (0),\quad F\to c_3\ (1.414<8.944,8.544)
\end{align*}
\textbf{Update:}
\[
c_1=(1,1.5),\qquad c_2=(8,1.5),\qquad c_3=(4.5,8.5)
\]
\textbf{Iteration 2 — Assign (using new centroids):} Every point remains closest to its own cluster's new centroid (each point is only $0.5$ or $0.707$ from its own centroid, while the nearest other centroid is several units away). No further changes.\\
\boxed{\text{Converged: }\{A,B\}\to(1,1.5),\ \{C,D\}\to(8,1.5),\ \{E,F\}\to(4.5,8.5)}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.68]
\draw[->,thick] (-0.5,0) -- (9.5,0) node[right]{\small $x_1$};
\draw[->,thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,10) node[above]{\small $x_2$};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (1,1) circle (3pt) node[below,font=\scriptsize]{$A$};
\filldraw[primaryblue] (1,2) circle (3pt) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{$B$};
\filldraw[accentorange] (8,1) circle (3pt) node[below,font=\scriptsize]{$C$};
\filldraw[accentorange] (8,2) circle (3pt) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{$D$};
\filldraw[darkgreen] (4,8) circle (3pt) node[below,font=\scriptsize]{$E$};
\filldraw[darkgreen] (5,9) circle (3pt) node[above,font=\scriptsize]{$F$};
\draw[primaryblue,thick] (1,1.5) circle (5pt);
\draw[accentorange,thick] (8,1.5) circle (5pt);
\draw[darkgreen,thick] (4.5,8.5) circle (5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\textbf{Practical Notes on k-Means:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item At every iteration, check \textbf{both} whether any point changed cluster \textbf{and} whether centroids moved — stop only when neither happens
\item Poorly chosen initial centroids (Example 3.14) can cause extra iterations or a worse final clustering; k-Means is often run several times with different random starts and the best result kept
\item Unlike Decision Trees or Naive Bayes, k-Means never uses class labels — the "correct" grouping is discovered purely from distances
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Sum of Squared Errors and Choosing k}

A cluster contains the points $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$, $(1,3)$. Compute the centroid and the SSE for this cluster.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
c &= \left(\frac{0+2+1}{3},\frac{0+0+3}{3}\right) = (1,1) \\[4pt]
d((0,0),c)^2 &= 1+1 = 2 \\
d((2,0),c)^2 &= 1+1 = 2 \\
d((1,3),c)^2 &= 0+4 = 4 \\[4pt]
SSE &= 2+2+4
\end{align*}
\boxed{SSE = 8}

For the points $(1,1)$, $(1,2)$, $(5,5)$, $(5,6)$, compute the SSE when using $k=1$ (one cluster containing all points) and compare it to the SSE when using $k=2$ (clusters $\{(1,1),(1,2)\}$ and $\{(5,5),(5,6)\}$).

\textbf{Solution:}
\textbf{Case $k=1$:} centroid $=\left(\frac{1+1+5+5}{4},\frac{1+2+5+6}{4}\right)=(3,3.5)$
\begin{align*}
SSE_{k=1} &= [(1-3)^2+(1-3.5)^2]+[(1-3)^2+(2-3.5)^2]+[(5-3)^2+(5-3.5)^2]+[(5-3)^2+(6-3.5)^2] \\
&= [4+6.25]+[4+2.25]+[4+2.25]+[4+6.25]
\end{align*}
\boxed{SSE_{k=1} = 33}

\textbf{Case $k=2$:} centroids $(1,1.5)$ and $(5,5.5)$
\begin{align*}
SSE_{k=2} &= [(1-1)^2+(1-1.5)^2]+[(1-1)^2+(2-1.5)^2]+[(5-5)^2+(5-5.5)^2]+[(5-5)^2+(6-5.5)^2] \\
&= 0.25+0.25+0.25+0.25
\end{align*}
\boxed{SSE_{k=2} = 1}
Increasing $k$ from 1 to 2 dropped the SSE sharply, from 33 down to 1, because each point now sits much closer to its own (smaller) cluster's centroid.

