A Self-Organizing Map is an unsupervised neural network that arranges a grid of \textbf{neurons} (each holding a weight vector of the same dimension as the input) so that neurons which are close together on the grid end up responding to similar input patterns. Unlike k-Means, which produces unordered clusters, a SOM preserves \textbf{topology} — nearby neurons on the map represent nearby regions of the input space.

This is similar in spirit to k-Means (each neuron's weight vector acts like a centroid), except the neurons also influence their grid-neighbours during training, which keeps similar patterns mapped near each other.

