\subsection{Best Matching Unit and Weight Update}

Three neurons have weight vectors $w_1=(1,1)$, $w_2=(4,4)$, $w_3=(8,1)$. For input $x=(5,3)$, find the BMU.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
d(x,w_1) &= \sqrt{(5-1)^2+(3-1)^2} = \sqrt{16+4} = \sqrt{20} \approx 4.472 \\
d(x,w_2) &= \sqrt{(5-4)^2+(3-4)^2} = \sqrt{1+1} = \sqrt{2} \approx 1.414 \\
d(x,w_3) &= \sqrt{(5-8)^2+(3-1)^2} = \sqrt{9+4} = \sqrt{13} \approx 3.606
\end{align*}
The smallest distance is $d(x,w_2)\approx1.414$.
\boxed{\text{BMU} = w_2}

Using the BMU $w_2=(4,4)$ found in Example 3.19 and input $x=(5,3)$, update $w_2$ with learning rate $\eta=0.5$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
w_2^{\text{new}} &= w_2^{\text{old}} + \eta(x-w_2^{\text{old}}) \\
&= (4,4) + 0.5\big[(5,3)-(4,4)\big] \\
&= (4,4) + 0.5(1,-1) \\
&= (4,4) + (0.5,-0.5)
\end{align*}
\boxed{w_2^{\text{new}} = (4.5, 3.5)}

Using the same setup as Example 3.20, suppose neuron $w_3=(8,1)$ is also inside the BMU's neighbourhood. Update $w_3$ with the same input $x=(5,3)$ and learning rate $\eta=0.5$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
w_3^{\text{new}} &= w_3^{\text{old}} + \eta(x-w_3^{\text{old}}) \\
&= (8,1) + 0.5\big[(5,3)-(8,1)\big] \\
&= (8,1) + 0.5(-3,2) \\
&= (8,1) + (-1.5,1)
\end{align*}
\boxed{w_3^{\text{new}} = (6.5, 2)}
Notice that $w_3$ still moves toward $x$, but starts farther away and so remains farther from $x$ than the BMU does — this is expected since it is only a neighbour, not the BMU itself.

k-Nearest Neighbor is an \textbf{instance-based} (lazy) learning algorithm. It stores all the training examples and performs no explicit training phase. To classify a new point, it finds the $k$ training points closest to it (using a distance measure such as Euclidean distance) and assigns the \textbf{majority class} among those $k$ neighbors.

Unlike Decision Trees or Naive Bayes, which build a model in advance, k-NN waits until a new point actually needs to be classified — this is why it is called a \textit{lazy} learner.

